{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3176","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3176","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3176\",\"parent_revision\":\"32ca303a44bb1fbf0ef761a943dd262313a2f7f753e3f86aabbe88e9dbc71af4\",\"root_revision\":\"32ca303a44bb1fbf0ef761a943dd262313a2f7f753e3f86aabbe88e9dbc71af4\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nImports aliased to underscore-prefixed names (`from marko.block import Heading as _MarkoHeading`, `import re as _re`, `from . import models as _models`) are widespread across the codebase, and no lint rule or review gate stops another one being added.\\n\\nWhy it matters, per the repo owner: we control every part of this system, so a private-namespace alias buys nothing. An import is already module-scoped, so the `_` prefix adds no encapsulation. It hides the real symbol name at every use site, breaks grep for that symbol, and is a signal that a module has grown a private-namespace hack in place of proper structure. Recurring instances of it are a code smell to be treated as an architectural signal rather than glossed over.\\n\\nMeasured scope on 2026-08-23 (repo-wide, excluding graphify-out and .venv):\\n- 138 occurrences across 66 files\\n- roughly 70 of those in non-test production code\\n\\nConcentrations worth noting:\\n- `backlog_core/server.py` aliases stdlib modules to underscore names \u2014 `import re as _re`, `import os as _os`, `import json as _json`, `import time as _time`, `import logging as _logging`, `import difflib as _difflib`\\n- `from . import models as _models` appears in `parsing.py`, `operations.py`, `artifact_migration.py`\\n- `from backlog_core import rendering as _rendering` appears in five backend modules\\n- `import dh_paths as _dh_paths` appears in at least seven modules\\n- `sam_schema/core/models.py` aliases `datetime as _dt` and `Path as _Path`\\n\\nTwo related smells surfaced alongside it in `backlog_core/parsing.py` and are probably worth including in the same sweep:\\n- third-party imports placed mid-file rather than in the module's import block (the marko imports sit around line 814 of ~1400)\\n- function-local imports carrying `# ruff: ignore[import-outside-top-level]` suppressions used to paper over a genuine import cycle rather than breaking the cycle\\n\\nImpact if left: every new module copies the surrounding convention, so the count grows; symbol-level grep stays unreliable for common names across the plugin; and the modules with the heaviest aliasing are the same ones that keep accumulating structural defects, which suggests the aliasing is tracking a real structural problem rather than being cosmetic.\\n\\nThere is currently no automated guard. Ruff's `flake8-import-conventions` (ICN) enforces specific conventional aliases and does not catch this shape, and `PLR0402` covers a different pattern. Whether a guard is feasible, and what form it takes, is grooming work \u2014 noting only that without one this reverts.\\n\\nUser-provided context: the fix direction the repo owner stated is to use the real names and, where a genuine collision exists, to move shared symbols into their own module rather than aliasing around the collision. A partial fix is already landing in `backlog_core/parsing.py` and `backlog_core/entry_blocks.py` as part of PR #3165; this item covers the remaining files and the missing guard.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Repo owner call-out during PR #3165 review response, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"31714e84bd44280dc90d9cff5bc2be5f485a1a66f502139a133c879a8b991629\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQPWtqg\"}"}